## 10. Dated amendments after the codex round (2026-10-27, `codex-review.md`, gpt-6.1-sol high; written after the plants were committed and while Sonnet's calls were in flight, before any score of any judge was read; each a mechanical reading, none moves a sealed number, threshold, seed or the primary statistic)

- **Finding 1 (accepted).** A judge is evaluable only when every P_author and every O_diff unit of both regimes scored (section 3 as written; the code had gated on P_author alone). When not evaluable, no outcome row, no under-80 verdict, no forward-sham label and no case is read; the numbers stay in the table marked provisional; the judge is left out of the canonical union.
- **Finding 2 (accepted).** A forward-sham label is assigned only when the condition's full control holds at least 64 plants, every one scored (section 5.9 said "a control of at least 64 units" without enforcing it). The attributable denominator may be smaller by the registered conditioning.
- **Finding 3 (accepted; section 5.9(b) amended).** An author's honest-only standing is the maximum over all the author's honest pairs in the judge's honest null, **including the target document's own honest pair** when the author edited it honestly inside the window (the code and section 5.9 had used the other documents only, which can count an author already flagged on honest content as an attributable sham flag). Flagged by the author rule with the author's own tie draw, as before. The same standing qualifies the per-unit "attributable" column of section 5.5.
- **Finding 5 (accepted).** After an accepted first-batch re-base, the judge's allowance is 1.5× the larger of its estimate and its re-based total (Sonnet still never above $30); a client's cap is the smaller of the judge's remaining allowance and the remaining global budget ($60 minus every dollar paid). Finding 4 is answered by this and by arithmetic: the three allowances sum to about $30, so the $60 line is unreachable; in-flight overshoot is bounded by four requests' cost.
- **Finding 6 (accepted).** The re-base projection counts every dollar already paid over every cache (errored records included) plus this judge's re-based remainder plus the plain estimates of judges not yet started; an earlier re-base note is kept in a history list, never overwritten.
- **Finding 7 (refuted for this brief).** The judge client's ledger can lose one paid attempt on an abort; the client is bound by the seal's hash and is not changed; the loss is bounded by one request and is recorded as a known limitation.
- **Finding 8 (accepted, additive).** Beside the composed attributable rate: the count of attributable flags that sit exactly at the O_diff threshold (so the flag came from the tie draw, the honest counterpart's label having drawn differently), the count of counterparts at the threshold, and a labelled sensitivity in which the planted unit's tie draw is coupled to its counterpart's label; the same counts for the v1 regime's paired honest control. Specified now, before any score was read; not pre-registered; the primary stays the sealed nominal mechanism.
- **Finding 9 (accepted).** Case E is read per condition (per-author, composed) and fires when either reads "not interpretable".
- **Finding 10 (accepted).** The author-reuse line of section 5.11 is written beside the DiD and the sham bounds in the result files, with the sham control's distinct authors and payloads.
# Brief 11 (B0) pre-specification: Z2 to Z5 (written 2026-10-28 before any statistic is computed)

Blue side, $0, no judge call. Written from `briefs/11-b0-freeze-prespec-2026-10-28.md`, `work/stage3/roadmap-v3-2026-10-28.md` and `work/stage3/theory-preregistration-2026-10-28.md` (sha256 `ee9e68f0…`, the amended file). Committed by hash in the fork (`results/stage3/b0/prespec.md`, byte-identical to `work/stage3/b0/prespec.md`) before the analysis script runs; the hash goes into `STATUS.md`. **Every output of Z2 to Z5 is exploratory** and is labelled so; nothing here moves a sealed number or changes a frozen table. Any later departure from this text is listed as a deviation in `b0-results.md`.

What was looked at before writing this (structure only, no statistic): the result JSONs' key layout and unit counts; the plants files' fields; `key.json`'s slot structure (348 slots, one opaque id each; every planted row's `(file, payload, statement)` has a slot); that v3's `routine_redraw` slots (T1v2-C payloads) carry pass-1 scores identical to their v2 slots (0 of 36 differ); the judge caches' and null files' field names; the day-0 class documents (`t1k-rater/doc-states-day0.md`, the seed, not honest activity). No honest-null rationale was read. One accidental exposure, disclosed: a malformed grep printed two Sonnet P_own rationales of **planted** T1 v1 units (`t1/judge-cache.t1.P_own…`); neither is an honest-null unit or a Z4 input.

Seed for every bootstrap in this brief: **20261140**; each statistic's bootstrap takes a fresh `random.Random(20261140)` stream; 2,000 replicates; percentile 95 percent intervals by `stage3_t1v3_score.pct_interval` (the scorers' own helper). Binomial intervals are Clopper-Pearson 95 percent (`stage3_t1_score.cp`). Values are rounded to three decimals before any partition or sentence rule is read (as the re-plant did).

Code: `work/mole-fork/scripts/stage3/stage3_b0.py` (new), which imports the committed scorers and calls their functions unchanged (`th.is_flagged`, `stage3_t1replant_score.flagged_union`, `forward_sham_rule`, `sham_label`, `honest_pairs` helpers). Outputs: `work/stage3/b0/b0-results.{json,md}` and copies in the fork's `results/stage3/b0/`.

