codex
1. **Blocker — Incomplete plantings can produce substantive conclusions.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:619](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:619), [line 681](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:681), [line 839](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:839). Unscored units become false flags through `payload_flags`; Δ and its partition are computed before admission. The Markdown prints that partition even when inadmissible, and `floor_sentence` ignores evaluability entirely. With **every planted record missing**, I reproduced `partition = excluded` and “B4’s per-agent side is moot.” **Fix:** preserve unknown flags, suppress partition labels until all gates pass, and pre-write a “floor not evaluated” sentence for incomplete views.

2. **Blocker — $60 is not a hard spending cap.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:388](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:388), [judge_client.py:470](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/judge_client.py:470). The budget arithmetic is correct, but the client checks only dollars already paid. A request can start with less remaining budget than it costs; concurrent requests can all pass before their costs arrive. Even serial execution can overshoot. **Fix:** reserve a conservative maximum request cost atomically before dispatch, including retries, and reconcile afterward. Independently running invocations also need a shared reservation mechanism or enforced serialization.

3. **Important — A failed $50 re-base does not bind subsequent invocations.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:333](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:333), [line 460](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:460). `allowance` and `planned_total` fall back to the original estimate when `continue` is false. `plants` checks for a null record but never checks the estimate’s licence or re-base stops. Another judge’s null invocation can also start after a recorded stop. **Fix:** enforce a persistent run-level stop in every spending entry point; require the applicable accepted re-base and reject stale null records after a stop.

4. **Important — The window “reading” contradicts the brief and changes the comparison.** [plan.md:22](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:22), [stage3_b1_dossier.py:155](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:155). The brief explicitly requires O_diff’s planting boundary. The dossier instead always starts at day 0 and ends on April 8; O_diff ends on composition day. This affects **54/128 re-plants, 66/152 v2 plants, and 61/128 v3 plants** at the starting boundary alone. Later honest edits can also erase or change statements after O_diff evaluated them—as the named v2 example demonstrates. **Fix:** obtain a prospective specification amendment for this changed estimand, or implement matching windows with an explicitly matched null. Calling this a mechanical reading is insufficient.

5. **Important — Stopping one reading changes the brief’s stopping rule.** [plan.md:58](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:58), [stage3_b1_dossier.py:474](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:474). The brief says “stop that judge”; the plan explicitly continues its other reading. Treating each reading as an instrument is scientifically defensible, but it is a substantive exception to the specified stopping rule. **Fix:** amend the brief prospectively or stop both readings when either null is defective.

6. **Important — Global counterpart matching can delete an unrelated line and misplace the insertion.** [stage3_author_doc.py:150](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_author_doc.py:150). Candidates come from **every** unmatched isolated line; locality only breaks similarity ties. I reproduced an edit to an appended neighbour line removing a similar, unrelated earlier line, then inserting its replacement at that earlier position. An author-only statement absent from the live `before` state is also eligible. The 0.6 choice is label-blind, but a library default does not establish correct correspondence. `check_replay` does not validate this: it resets to each actual `before` state, avoiding the disputed counterfactual. **Fix:** track structural line identities, constrain counterparts to justified regions, and classify ambiguous rebases explicitly.

7. **Important — “No other author’s line can enter” is not a general invariant.** [stage3_author_doc.py:140](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_author_doc.py:140), [plan.md:28](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:28). SequenceMatcher’s insertion opcodes are not evidence of original authorship. With live `before = [head, NEIGHBOUR, x1, x2]`, a whole write moving `NEIGHBOUR` after `x2` inserts that unchanged neighbour line into an isolated state initially containing only `[head, x1, x2]`. I reproduced this offline. **Fix:** preserve provenance across moves and distinguish genuinely introduced text from copied or relocated text. Narrow the guarantee until that is implemented.

8. **Important — A scored packet is accepted even when rendering is defective.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:619](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:619). Planting evaluability checks only `outcome == "scored"`. It ignores packet truncation and the isolated leak check that the plan says must be zero. Consequently, a truncated or leaking packet can receive sham and partition labels. **Fix:** validate current rendered packets before scoring and again before analysis; make rendering defects disqualifying. The current dry run reports no truncation or isolated exact-line exposure, but those are recorded observations rather than enforced gates.

9. **Important — Results bypass instrument and cache identity checks.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:779](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:779), [line 298](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:298). Results load raw JSONL records without `make_client`’s validation. Recorded plan, module and prompt hashes are not compared across estimate, null and results. A system-only prompt change can leave packet keys unchanged, allowing old scores to be reported under the current plan hash. **Fix:** centralize strict artifact binding and use it in every mode, including results; compare plan, prompts, modules, manifests and request settings.

10. **Important — Re-base projections undercount separately paid failed attempts on reruns.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:431](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:431), [line 364](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:364). The sample mean uses the latest returned record per unit. Earlier errored records remain in paid dollars, but are absent from that mean. Subtracting *all* paid dollars from the estimated completion total then treats those failed-attempt costs as completed planned work. **Fix:** project outstanding units separately and add all paid costs, or explicitly account for retry overhead. Ordinary successfully cached records retain their original costs correctly.

11. **Important — Accepted null refusals become apparently clean honest standing.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:396](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:396), [line 492](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:492). Four refusals among 89 units pass 11.12(d); I verified this. Those pairs disappear from calibration maxima and `honest_pairs`. Forward-sham attribution then treats an unavailable honest target score as absent evidence of flagging, rather than unknown standing. This can alter both calibration and admission. **Fix:** pre-register treatment of unresolved honest scores and propagate uncertainty into attribution; withhold a reading when plausible missing scores change its conclusion.

12. **Important — Live-minus-isolated recall does not identify the neighbour leak’s causal size.** [plan.md:107](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:107), [stage3_b1_dossier.py:725](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:725). The readings differ in surrounding honest content, survival of the author’s own lines, framing, and separately calibrated thresholds. Their recall difference combines all those effects. Positive exposure establishes that neighbour text is available, not how much it caused recall to rise. The quoted exposure also pools attack and sham units while prediction 3 uses `v1` only. **Fix:** call this the paired live-versus-isolated view effect, identify the exposure population explicitly, and reserve causal attribution for a controlled comparison.

13. **Important — Several outcome sentences claim more than their conditions establish.** [plan.md:89](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:89), [line 113](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:113). “Present” requires a point estimate ≥0.3, not a lower bound ≥0.3; “attenuated” can have an upper bound above 0.3. These establish neither a benefit definitely ≥0.3 nor definitely smaller than it. Likewise, below headroom is not necessarily a recall floor: **15/24 detected versus 0/24 sham** fails headroom, yet triggers “B4 … moot.” **Fix:** retain the prescribed partition labels while explicitly describing point estimates and uncertainty; prospectively distinguish insufficient headroom from a floor. This requires amending the controlling sentences, not merely changing code.

14. **Minor — The paired leak readings specified for v2/v3 are missing.** [plan.md:76](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/stage3/b1/plan.md:76), [stage3_b1_dossier.py:793](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:793). Section 6 applies per planting, but paired live-minus-isolated estimates are computed only through re-plant prediction 3. V2/v3 receive judge-free exposure counts only. **Fix:** implement the pre-registered paired readings with explicit regime pooling and outcome templates, or narrow section 6 prospectively.

15. **Minor — B1 drops the inherited dependence disclosure.** [stage3_b1_dossier.py:642](/Users/aravind/KruxAI/tais-project-selection/work/checks/stage3_b1_dossier.py:642). `forward_sham_rule` returns the warning that 64 plants reuse 24 payloads and a small author pool; B1 discards it. Binomial bounds and payload bootstraps do not capture shared-author dependence, so their nominal coverage should not be presented without that limitation. **Fix:** preserve the disclosure and distinct payload/author counts; label any dependence-aware alternative as a sensitivity.

**Checked and found correct**

